Every error about a missing or malformed option prints the command that would
have worked, built from the arguments the user actually typed -- never a
generic example with a placeholder id.

## The case that prompted this

An agent moving card 79, four calls for one move:

  karr move 79 in-progress            -> "Status 'in-progress' requires --claim"
  karr move 79 in-progress --claim    -> "Option claim requires an argument" + usage
  karr move --help | head -30         -> read the usage
  karr move 79 in-progress --claim main-agent   -> worked

Two separate faults:

1. "requires --claim" names the option but not that a value follows, so the
   agent typed `--claim` bare. The message provoked the second error.
2. The second error did carry the answer (`--claim=String`, high up in a
   15-line usage block), but the agent had piped through `tail -3` -- karr
   output is long, agents truncate it -- and saw only the -h/--help/--man
   lines.

## What to build

Replace the bare requirement with the corrected invocation:

  Status 'in-progress' requires a claim:
    karr move 79 in-progress --claim NAME

Real id, real status, the caller's own words. Only the value the user has to
supply stays a placeholder.

Placement matters as much as content: the actionable line goes LAST. Agents
pipe karr through `tail -n`, so anything printed before a long usage block is
what gets cut. Today it is exactly backwards.

## Scope

Start with the commands an agent meets rarely -- move, handoff, needs,
materialize, import, repair -- where nothing is memorised and every message is
read cold. The frequent ones (list, show, create) can follow.

Out of scope, deliberately: --json output is untouched, and the exit-code
contract of ADR 0002 stands. No environment sniffing is needed here -- a
message that shows the working command serves a human equally well. Detecting
an AI caller only becomes a question for output a human would find noisy, and
that is a separate ticket.

2026-08-28 12:21 Implemented in the working tree, not committed. Both faults are closed.

Fault 1 (content): App::karr::Error/command_hint is the one renderer for the suggestion line; clean_error had to learn to carry it through the batch runner's first-line reduction, or every move failure would have shown the colon with the answer cut off. require_claim now says "requires a claim:" plus the working line, through claim_hint_tokens on Role::TaskMutation (default karr edit ID --status STATUS, overridden in Cmd::Move). move, needs, import, materialize and repair covered; "No karr board found" unified across all five commands that raise it.

Fault 2 (placement): Role::ExitCodes buffers STDERR for the length of new_with_options and prints the diagnostic AFTER the usage block with the suggestion under it. Two writers had to be caught, which is why a $SIG{__WARN__} handler alone was not enough -- Getopt::Long warns, but a required=>1 option missing is reported by MooX::Options with a bare print STDERR that no warn handler sees. The placeholder comes from the command's own usage_string, so both messages about --claim say NAME.

Unknown option deliberately gets the reordering and no suggestion: the only working line there is the caller's own minus the token they meant something by, and an agent reading tail -1 would have copied a state-changing move it never asked for.

